Key conceptual framing:
- MDGEN takes subsampled trajectories as training data. Subsampling (temporal sparse sampling, e.g., every K frames) removes high-frequency content above the Nyquist frequency of the subsampled sequence. Torsion angle oscillations (e.g., in flexible dihedrals of molecules like alanine dipeptide, butane, or small peptides) have periods in the range of sub-picosecond to picoseconds. If full MD is stored at e.g. 0.5 fs and subsampled to 10 fs, oscillations at 1-5 ps^-1 frequencies... Actually let's get numbers: torsional librations around a dihedral minimum have characteristic frequencies ~ tens of cm^-1 (i.e., 1-2 THz, periods ~0.5-1 ps). Transitions / rotation about bonds slower. Subsampling at 2-5 ps would completely lose these librations.

